# Line-ending policy.
#
# The repository is developed on Windows and built on Windows and Linux, so text files are pinned to LF in
# both the index and the working tree: a checkout on either platform then produces the same bytes, which is
# what keeps diffs, patches and acceptance evidence comparable between the two.
#
# Nothing here uses `text=auto`. A blanket normalization would also rewrite files that are deliberately
# byte-exact, and a policy change must not silently turn into a repository-wide diff.
#
# Deliberately NOT listed: `*.cmd`. `compile.cmd` and `clean.cmd` are single files that are both a POSIX
# shell script and a Windows batch file, so the two halves want opposite line endings - the shell half
# breaks with CRLF, and batch files are happiest with it. Today the index holds LF and a Windows checkout
# gets CRLF, which is exactly what makes both halves work; pinning either way would break one of them.

*.c             text eol=lf
*.cc            text eol=lf
*.cpp           text eol=lf
*.h             text eol=lf
*.hpp           text eol=lf
*.cmake         text eol=lf
*.cmake.in      text eol=lf
CMakeLists.txt  text eol=lf
*.qml           text eol=lf
*.qrc           text eol=lf
*.ui            text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.txt           text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.in            text eol=lf

# Recorded measurements are evidence: they must stay byte-exact, so no conversion is applied to them at
# all. A line-ending change inside a recorded host measurement would be a silent alteration of data.
/research/capture/evidence/*.json  -text -eol
